{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "The Importance of Extending UML"}, {"type": "position", "content": 6}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 2}, {"type": "level", "content": "A2.2"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear when you talk about communication diagrams:"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Describe common activities at work to a visitor using short sentences in familiar spoken and written situations.  Give detailed information related to UML and common related topics."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Identifies the expressions and vocabulary necessary for the description of places of work and jobs in common written and spoken situations.  Describes common activities related to Software Programming through the use of appropriate expressions, grammar and vocabulary in written or spoken situations according to the abilities related to this level."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob06re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "powerful "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "very strong, mighty or capable"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: UML is one of the most powerful tools software designers have."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob06aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "standardized "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "something that follows a set of standards or rules"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: It allows them to put together a set of instructions adapted to their own particular needs and even to understand each other by using diagrams that are more standardized than the ones they used in the past."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob06aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "be prone to "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to be inclined to something or to have a tendency"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Interesting because thanks to this feature we'll be less prone to confusions among designers, won't we?"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob06aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "argue "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to contend, to compete or to present a different point of view"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Some designers argue, particularly in business application development, that UML does not provide enough tools for them to work on a daily basis."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob06aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "truly "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "really, certainly or accurately"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adverb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I know for sure that a more robust UML would translate into a truly versatile set of tools."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob06aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "overcome "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to be able to get through difficulties, to solve  "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: It also says that UML will overcome communication and cultural barriers."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n4le02ob06aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for information about UML diagrams "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Prepare a presentation about why it is important to extend UML. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share the presentation with your teacher and classmates."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I look for information about UML. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I prepare a presentation about the importance of extending UML "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I discuss my presentation with my partners."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Simple Past, Simple Present, and Simple Future With Time Expression"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You can use simple tenses in English to refer to different actions that took place in different moments. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "UML is one of the most powerful tools software designers have."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Did you read the article I shared with you? It's great for the paper we need to write about UML and its development."}, {"type": "example", "content": "In the book, I imagine the author will present some suggestions to extend UML beyond object and component technology."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<strong>Simple Present</strong></br></br>  We use this tense to talk about facts and things that happen regularly, like routines or repeated actions. </br></br>  The structure that we use to write sentences using this tense is the following: </br></br>  <div class=\"center_text\"> Subject + main verb + complement.</div></br>It is important to remember that if you use the pronouns <i>he</i>, <i>she</i> and <i>it</i>, you need to add a final <b>\u201c-s\u201d</b> or <b>\u201c-es\u201d</b> to the verb. On the other hand, some common time expressions for the simple present tense are: <b>Regularly</b>, <b>every day</b>, <b>every week</b>, <b>every month</b>, <b>every year</b>, and <b>always</b>, among others. </br></br>  Example: <span class=\"italic\"></br> I <strong>like</strong> to review notes on the features of communication diagrams <b>every month</b>.</span> </br></br>  You can also make negative sentences using this structure: </br></br>  <div class=\"center_text\"> Subject + <strong>don't/doesn't</strong> + main verb + complement.</div></br>  Example: <span class=\"italic\"></br>  We <strong>don't use</strong> the notation from UML 1.x anymore. </br>She <b>does not like</b> to work with computers at all.</span> </br></br>  If you want to make yes/no questions using the simple present, you can follow this structure: </br></br>  <div class=\"center_text\"> Do/does + subject + main verb (base form) + complement + ?</div></br>  Example: </br><span class=\"italic\"><strong>Do</strong> you <strong>use</strong> UML at work every day? </span>   </br></br> You can also make open-ended questions by using this structure: </br></br>  <div class=\"center_text\"> Wh- question word + do/does + subject + main verb (base form) + complement + ?</div></br> Example: <span class=\"italic\"></br>  <strong>Why do</strong> you <strong>use</strong> UML every day?</span> </br></br>  <strong>Simple Past</strong></br></br>  We use this tense to talk about actions that started in the past and finished in the past as well. </br></br>  Remember that there are regular verbs (that add \u201c-ed\u201d to their past form) and irregular verbs (that have different changes). Common time expressions for the simple past are: <b>yesterday</b>, <b>last week</b>, <b>last month</b>, <b>last year</b>, among others. </br></br>  Examples: </br>  <span class=\"italic\"> We <strong>followed</strong> the diagram, but our boss decided to change it yesterday. </span> </br> We <strong>came</strong> to work last week and started using UML right away. </br></br>  You can make negative sentences by using the auxiliary verb <span class=\"italic\">did not</span> or <span class=\"italic\">didn't</span>. Take a look at the following example:</br></br><span class=\"italic\">  She <strong>didn't use </strong>the standard notation until I explained it to her.</span> </br>  You can make questions following this structure: </br></br> <div class=\"center_text\">(Wh- word) + did + subject + main verb (base form) + complement + ?</div></br>  Example: </br>  <span class=\"italic\"><strong>What did</strong> you <strong>learn</strong> about UML in your last lecture? </span> </br></br>Example:</br><i><b>What did</b> you do to learn this topic?</br><b>Did</b> you know about the importance of UML?</i></br></br> <strong>Simple Future</strong></br></br>  You use this tense to talk about actions or plans that will take place in the future or things that you haven't planned. Common time expressions for the future simple are: <b>Tomorrow</b>, <b>next week</b>, <b>next month</b>, <b>next year</b>, <b>soon</b>, among others. </br></br>Use this structure to make affirmative sentences: </br></br>  <div class=\"center_text\"> Subject + will + main verb (base form) + complement.</div></br>  Example: </br>  We <strong>will update</strong> the diagrams soon. </br></br>  In negative statements, you can use <i>will not</i> or <i>won't</i> using the following structure: </br></br> <div class=\"center_text\">  Subject + will not/won't + main verb (base form) + complement.</div></br> Example: <span class=\"italic\"></br>  They <strong>won't use</strong> this diagram for the following projects next year. </span> </br></br>  To make questions using the future simple, you can follow this structure: </br></br>  <div class=\"center_text\"> (Wh- word) + will + subject + main verb (base form) + complement + ? </div></br>  Example: </br> <span class=\"italic\"> <strong>What will</strong> you do to include IML in your project?</br><b>Will</b> we <strong>implement </strong>UML next year? </span>"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Quantifiers (Review)"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You can use <b>quantifiers</b> to talk about approximate amounts of something."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Interesting because thanks to this feature we'll be less prone to confusions among designers, won't we?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Some designers argue, particularly in business application development, that UML does not provide enough tools for them to work on a daily basis. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Especially because it is not completely uniform yet, there are some communication barriers between designers and there is not enough support for a wider approach to business application development."}, {"type": "text", "content": "When comparing quantities, you can use <b>less</b> to refer to a noun with a lower or reduced amount of something. </br></br>  Examples: </br><span class=\"italic\"> Complicated processes bring<strong> less</strong> earnings. </br> Internet tools offer <strong>less</strong> resources than regular programs.</span> </br></br>  You can use the quantifier <b>more</b> to make comparisons between nouns and to talk about an increasing and approximate quantity. Remember, the quantifier goes before the noun or adjective. </br></br>  Examples: </br> <span class=\"italic\">There are <strong>more</strong> responsibilities involved in organizing the right model. </br> With different modeling tools processes are <strong>more </strong>organized.</span> </br></br>  We use <span class=\"strong\">enough </span>in affirmative sentences. It means that the quantity is sufficient. In negative sentences, it means that the quantity is insufficient. It can be used with both, countable and uncountable nouns. </br></br>  Examples: </br> <span class=\"italic\">There aren't <strong>enough </strong>designers to finish the model today! </br> There are <strong>enough</strong> UML diagrams for our needs. </span></br></br>  Some other quantifiers include <span class=\"italic\">plenty of, some, none, lots of</span>, among others."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Parallelism in Writing"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "When writing, you need to make sure that ideas follow not only a logical order, but also that they are related to one another by maintaining parallelism."}, {"type": "text", "content": "In order to communicate a sequence of ideas, you need to maintain parallelism for your reader to be able to understand what you are trying to communicate more effectively. Parallelism in writing refers to the way different elements in phrases should be arranged to maintain cohesion and clarity.</br></br>  Example: </br> <span class=\"italic\">I enjoy designing software using UML, <strike>to read</strike> about technology and <strike>to study </strike>English. </span></br></br>  This phrase does not maintain parallelism because the different activities that are mentioned do not have the same grammatical structures. A parallel phrase would look like this. </br>  Example: </br>  <span class=\"italic\">I enjoy designing software using UML, reading about technology and studying English.</span> </br></br>  As you can see, the parallel phrase makes reading clearer and the transition between ideas more harmonic. Keep this in mind when composing texts!"}], "type": "learning_points"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "id": 0}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe7n4le02ob06re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<div align=\"justify\"><strong>Paul</strong>: Deborah, did you read the article I shared with you? It's great for the paper we need to write about UML and its development.</br>\n<strong>Deborah</strong>: I just saw some pictures and read a little part of it. </br>\n<strong>Paul</strong>: Let's check it again, then. </br>\n<strong>Deborah</strong>: Okay\u2026 \u201cUML is one of the most powerful tools software designers have. It allows them to put together a set of instructions adapted to their own particular needs and even to understand each other by using diagrams that are more standardized than the ones they used in the past.\u201d </br>"}, {"time": "36000", "image": "enpe7n4le02ob06re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<div align=\"justify\"><strong>Paul</strong>: Interesting, because thanks to this feature, we'll be less prone to confusions among designers, won't we? </br>\n<strong>Deborah</strong>: Uh huh. Some designers argue, particularly in business application development, that UML does not provide enough tools for them to work on a daily basis. Especially because it is not completely uniform yet; there are some communication barriers between designers and there is not enough support for a wider approach to business application development. </br>\n<strong>Paul</strong>: I find that quite exciting. In the book, I imagine the author will present some suggestions to extend UML beyond object and component technology. </br>"}, {"time": "79000", "image": "enpe7n4le02ob06re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<div align=\"justify\"> <strong>Deborah</strong>: I hope so. In marketing, for example, I know for sure that a more robust UML would translate into a truly versatile set of tools. Even though UML presents certain unity, more efforts need to take place to maintain it. There are some new approaches in business I know like (SOA) service-oriented architecture and transaction processing technologies. So, by adding some of these approaches to UML, it will certainly go far beyond object-based modeling. </br>\n<strong>Paul</strong>: Do you think so? </br>\n<strong>Deborah</strong>: Yeah. Let me scan the text quickly. It also says that UML will overcome communication and cultural barriers, that developers will have to adapt to using the newest tools and that tool vendors will need to be more efficient at presenting the advantages of using an even more complete UML."}], "coverimg": "enpe7n4le02ob06re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n4le02ob06re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "title": ""}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"video_src": "enpe7n4le02ob06re5vsc01", "images": [{"time": "0", "image": "", "id": 0, "text": ""}]}, "type": "video"}], "type": "video", "_data": [], "id": 2, "title": ""}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can read about the importance of extending UML."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can explain why extending the UML beyond object and component technology."}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can write a meaningful short text related to UML and related ideas."}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "How has your experience with UML been? Share your thoughts."}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "What would you change about the tools that UML provides?"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Welcome"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attempt_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "You have 2 tries"}, {"type": "text", "content": "you have 1 attempt left"}], "correct_feedback": "Correct", "incorrect_feedback": [{"type": "text", "content": "try again"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Maybe next time"}], "allowed_attempts": 2}}, {"type": "free_header_text", "content": "Ask your partner:"}, {"type": "free_type_text_area", "content": "We can make it even better!"}], "img_path": "", "id": 0}, {"stimuli_ids": [1], "template_override": 1, "questions": [{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}], "type": "question"}], "responses": [{"correct": 0, "selected": 0, "_data": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}], "id": 0}, {"correct": 1, "selected": 0, "_data": [{"type": "text", "content": ""}], "id": 1}], "id": 0}], "type": "STI", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Two coworkers are reading and talking about UML. "}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"type": "group_name", "content": "Content"}], "type": "group"}, {"type": "feedback", "content": {"attemp